results.raw:
Young UK women aged 18-34 are likely to respond most to salient calorie information that makes the comparison concrete. A "240 CALORIES" badge next to a "95 CALORIES" fruit pot gives a quick, direct reason to swap. Graphic medical imagery is more likely to trigger avoidance or reactance in this group than behaviour change, so the schemes that rely on it (A and F) should do less work. Plain text warnings (C) are easy to tune out, and the no-label control (B) changes little.

Points: E 38, D 24, A 18, C 8, F 6, B 6.

{"points": {"A": 18, "B": 6, "C": 8, "D": 24, "E": 38, "F": 6}, "most_ignored": "C", "why": "A plain black-and-white text warning is low-salience and easily tuned out, and it adds no concrete comparison that would change the choice at the vending machine."}
